  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/nemapteryx_nenga_en/",
      "html": "<p>Nemapteryx nenga is a catfish from India, formerly placed in the genus Arius. It is not a shark, despite the name \"freshwater shark\". It reaches about 30 cm and should be kept in large aquariums only.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/nemapteryx_nenga_en/",
      "html": "<p>These \"freshwater sharks\" live in the mouths of large rivers, so they can live in pure freshwater as well as in pure seawater.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/nemapteryx_nenga_en/",
      "html": "<p>Keeping them in brackish water with a salt content of 5-10 grams per litre is recommended.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/nemapteryx_nenga_en/",
      "html": "<p>These catfish should always be kept in groups (5-10 specimens). Singly kept specimens do not thrive very well.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/nemapteryx_nenga_en/",
      "html": "<p>As far as is known, all species of the Arius relatives are mouthbrooders. The male takes the eggs into his mouth until they hatch.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/nemapteryx_nenga_en/",
      "html": "<p>Because of its nicely shaped dorsal fin, the popular name \"whimple freshwater shark\" could be used.</p>"
    }
  ],
